# PRD: Native Qwen MTP integration for hipfire spec-decode

**Status:** Draft 2026-05-09. Branch: `feat/native-mtp` (alpineQ fork, off PR #222 kernel
fixes). PR #204 (hetero DFlash) closed without merge — see "Why this PRD" below.

**Audience:** Fresh model context with no session memory. This document is
self-contained: hardware, target model, all measured baselines, file layout,
gotchas, validation gates, exit criteria.

---

## TL;DR

Implement native Qwen3.6 Multi-Token Prediction (MTP) head support in hipfire
to replace the DFlash external-drafter path on long-context decode. Empirical
target: **45 tok/s on 16K Wikipedia canonical** (vs hipfire AR-only 39 tok/s,
vs hipfire DFlash hetero 33 tok/s). Number is not aspirational — it is what
llama.cpp's MTP path delivers on the same hardware/model/prompt today.

Native MTP weights ship inside Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B's safetensors (15 tensors,
~100 MB in mq4) but the hipfire quantizer currently strips them at
`crates/hipfire-quantize/src/main.rs:2520`. Unstrip + add a forward path +
wire spec-decode = ~1 month of focused work split across 5 small PRs.

## Why this PRD (lessons from the dead end)

The session that produced this branch tried to make DFlash hetero (drafter on
GPU 1, target on GPU 0) competitive at 16K. After three commits — kernel
j-partition (kept, PR #222), drafter sliding window (reverted as
net-negative, see "Pitfall #1"), DDTree+hetero wiring — the best achievable
DFlash hetero throughput at 16K Wikipedia prose is **33 tok/s**, vs AR-only
**39 tok/s**. Speculation was actively losing per-token. Math: cycle wall
75 ms × τ=1.45 → 30.2 ms/token > AR 25.6 ms/token.

Then we benchmarked llama.cpp + Qwen3.6 native MTP via
`/tmp/bench-prompts/run_llama_server.sh` with `--spec-type mtp
--spec-draft-n-max 8` on the same prompt md5 (`d21ebdd2e3204c1788a7f2681d60c38a`)
and got **45.4 ± 0.2 tok/s** steady-state (5 runs). Acceptance rate 0.44 ×
n_max 8 → effective τ ≈ 3.5. On a code prompt (`/tmp/code_prompt.txt`,
HumanEval-style): **62 tok/s**, τ ≈ 4.5.

The DFlash approach is structurally weaker because the z-lab drafter we use
is 5 layers trained externally; Qwen team's native MTP is 1 layer trained
properly with their data and compute. Smaller drafter, higher τ. Switching
to native MTP is the single highest-ROI change available.

We also tested Lucebox PR #26 / hipfire issue #78 (target-side sliding-window
FA) on its existing branch `feat/sliding-window-fa`. At 16K context the
claimed 3.48× win does not materialize — all window sizes (W=128 / 512 / 2048
/ full) give 38.4–38.8 tok/s. Lucebox's win shows up at 60K+ where target FA
dominates over weight reads; at 16K the model is still weight-bandwidth-bound,
not attention-compute-bound. **Do not pursue #78 for our 16K workload.**

